            <ArticleTitle>New therapies in chronic prostatitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>313-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The optimal management of category III prostatitis (chronic pelvic pain syndrome) is not known. Conventional therapy usually consists of prolonged courses of antibiotics; however, clinical trials have never shown their efficacy. Newer therapies with some evidence for efficacy include alpha-blockers, anti-inflammatory phytotherapy (quercetin, bee pollen), physiotherapy, neuroleptics, and others with unique actions such as antinanobacterial treatment. A stepwise approach involving multiple treatment modalities is often successful for patients with this common and frustrating condition.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
